    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `triage` in `app/worker.py`, and all 5 tests pass (the 2 existing ones and 3 new ones).\n\n**How it works:** it makes a single call to `await client.messages.with_raw_response.create(...)` with `MODEL`, `max_tokens=512`, `SYSTEM` and the ticket as the only user message. The answer, `tokens_remaining` and `request_id` all come from that one response, so each ticket costs exactly one request. It returns the same keys as `ask_with_limits`, plus `\"throttle\": tokens_remaining < LOW_WATERMARK`.\n\n**One difference from the sync version:** in the installed `anthropic==1.8.0`, the async client's raw response is an `AsyncAPIResponse`, whose `parse()` is a coroutine. So the code uses `message = await raw.parse()`. Copying the sync line `raw.parse()` as-is would return a coroutine object instead of the message. The headers are still read the same way as in the sync version. I checked this in both the Context7 docs and the installed package source.\n\n**Tests (`tests/test_worker.py`):** these run `triage` against a mocked HTTP transport, so no network is needed. They check that:\n- exactly one request is sent, with the expected model, `max_tokens`, system prompt and message\n- the returned dict is correct\n- `throttle` is True just below `LOW_WATERMARK` and False exactly at it\n\nThis SDK version runs on `httpx2` rather than `httpx`, so the mock uses `httpx2.MockTransport`, which is already installed as an SDK dependency. I didn't add or change any dependencies."
